<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yekuno Amlak</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1270">1270</a>–<a href="/years/1285">1285</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomonic-dynasty">Solomonic dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yekuno Amlak was an emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> who seized power from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagwe-dynasty">Zagwe dynasty</a> in 1270. He founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomonic-dynasty">Solomonic dynasty</a>, which claimed descent from King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-of-sheba">Queen of Sheba</a> and ruled Ethiopia, with interruptions, until 1974. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire</a> treat his accession as a foundation of the medieval Ethiopian state.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yekuno-amlak.jpg" alt="Yekuno Amlak" width="320" height="268" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yekuno_Amlak.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Yekuno Amlak came from an old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amhara">Amhara</a> family and ruled locally in Geshen and Ambassel, around the Lake Hayq region, where he was educated at the Istifanos Monastery. He came from Bete Amhara, in parts of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wollo">Wollo</a> and northern Shewa. Medieval texts written in support of his dynasty claimed that he descended in the male line from the royal house of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksumite-empire">Aksum</a>, which was itself said to descend from the biblical King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a>. On that basis his dynasty claimed that he restored a Solomonic line and cast the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagwe-dynasty">Zagwe</a> kings as usurpers. His descendants kept up the claim, and the 1955 Ethiopian constitution enshrined it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Later hagiographies say that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tekle-haymanot-saint">Tekle Haymanot</a> raised him and helped him depose the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagwe-dynasty">Zagwe</a> king; earlier ones name Iyasus Mo'a, the abbot of Istifanos. G. W. B. Huntingford explains the difference by the later rise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/debre-libanos">Debre Libanos</a> over Istifanos. Neither tradition is contemporary with the people involved. Tradition adds that the Zagwe king Za-Ilmaknun imprisoned him on Mount Malot, and that he escaped, gathered support in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amhara">Amhara</a> provinces and Shewa, and defeated the Zagwe king at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ansata">Battle of Ansata</a>. Taddesse Tamrat argued that this king was Yetbarak, while Getatchew Mekonnen Hasen names Na'akueto La'ab.</p><p>As emperor he is said to have campaigned against the Kingdom of Damot, south of the Abbay River, and he founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ankober">Ankober</a> as an alternative capital near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">Emirs</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harar">Harar</a>, with whom his relations were friendly. He wrote to the Mamluk Sultan Baibars in 1273 asking for a new Abuna for the Ethiopian church; a letter survives, and it suggests the request was not his first. E. A. Wallis Budge states that he exchanged letters with the Byzantine Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-viii-palaiologos">Michael VIII</a> and sent him several giraffes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Yekuno Amlak died on 19 June 1285. He is credited with building the Church of Gennete Maryam near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lalibela">Lalibela</a>, which contains the earliest surviving dateable wall paintings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>. His descendant Emperor Baeda Maryam I had his body reburied in the church of Atronsa Maryam. He was the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomonic-dynasty">Solomonic dynasty</a>, which remained in power until 1974.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yekuno Amlak” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Solomonic dynasty was established in 1270, traditionally on 10 August, when <b>Yekuno Amlak</b> overthrew the last Zagwe king at the Battle of Ansata.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…and conquered the Sultanate of Shewa; in 1288 Sultan Wali Asma imposed his rule on Hubat, Zeila and the other Muslim states of the region, a consolidation of the Muslim lands that Taddesse Tamrat compares with Emperor <b>Yekuno Amlak</b>'s consolidation of the Christian highlands in the same years.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sultanate-of-ifat">Sultanate of Ifat · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1270 <b>Yekuno Amlak</b>, a noble from Amhara claiming descent from Solomon and the Aksumite kings, overthrew the last Zagwe king, founding the Solomonic line of the Ethiopian Empire.</blockquote><a href="/state/zagwe-dynasty">Zagwe dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
